const enBody = `<h1 id="top">Gemini 4 Hydrogen API: $1.50/$7.50, 1M Context, No Price Cliff</h1>

<p>On <strong>November 12, 2026</strong>, Google made <code>gemini-4-hydrogen</code> generally available. It is the fast, cheap member of the Gemini 4 family, and its headline rate is <strong>$1.50 per million input tokens and $7.50 per million output tokens</strong>, with a unified 1,048,576-token context window and four thinking levels.</p>

<p>That price is unremarkable on its own. What makes it worth reading is what happens when you line it up against the rest of Google's catalog: <strong>Hydrogen is the only Gemini 4 model whose published rate never expires.</strong> Gemini 4 Carbon and Gemini 4 Argon both double on December 1, 2026. Gemini 3.8 Flash and Gemini 3.6 Flash both double on January 1, 2027. Hydrogen's number is the number you will be paying in 2027.</p>

<p>There is a second coincidence hiding in the price card, and it is more useful than the first. <strong>Gemini 4 Hydrogen is priced at exactly Gemini 3.8 Flash's January 1, 2027 list rate</strong> — $1.50 in, $7.50 out, with the same $0.075 cached-input rate. Hydrogen is not a cheaper way to buy the old model. It is the old model's 2027 price, shipped today, with a better context architecture attached.</p>

<h2 id="price-card">The price card, tier by tier</h2>

<p>Google's pricing page publishes Hydrogen at four paid tiers plus a free tier. Here is the complete card, with the sibling models for comparison. Every rate below is per 1M tokens in USD, transcribed from Google's live pricing page on November 13, 2026.</p>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Model</th><th>Input</th><th>Output</th><th>Cached input</th><th>Free tier</th><th>Price change</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Gemini 4 Hydrogen</strong></td><td>$1.50</td><td>$7.50</td><td>$0.075</td><td>Yes</td><td><strong>None</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Gemini 4 Carbon</td><td>$2.00 &rarr; $4.00</td><td>$10.00 &rarr; $20.00</td><td>$0.10 &rarr; $0.20</td><td>No</td><td>Dec 1, 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gemini 4 Argon</td><td>$2.00 &rarr; $4.00</td><td>$10.00 &rarr; $20.00</td><td>$0.10 &rarr; $0.20</td><td>No</td><td>Dec 1, 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gemini 3.8 Flash</td><td>$0.75 &rarr; $1.50</td><td>$3.75 &rarr; $7.50</td><td>$0.075 &rarr; $0.15</td><td>Yes</td><td>Jan 1, 2027</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gemini 3.6 Flash</td><td>$0.75 &rarr; $1.50</td><td>$3.75 &rarr; $7.50</td><td>$0.075 &rarr; $0.15</td><td>Yes</td><td>Jan 1, 2027</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite</td><td>$0.30</td><td>$2.50</td><td>$0.03</td><td>Yes</td><td>None</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Two things in that table decide most deployments. First, <strong>Hydrogen is flat while its neighbors move</strong> — after December 1, Carbon and Argon cost 2.7x what Hydrogen costs, not 1.33x. Second, <strong>Hydrogen is the only frontier-tier Gemini 4 model on the free tier</strong>: Carbon and Argon have no free tier at any date, while Hydrogen and the Flash line are free of charge, rate-limited.</p>

<p>The rest of the tier ladder is standard. Batch and Flex both charge 50% of Standard: $0.75 input and $3.75 output. Priority charges 1.8x Standard: $2.70 input and $13.50 output. Grounding with Google Search and Google Maps each include 5,000 free requests per month, shared across Gemini 3 and newer models, then $14 per 1,000 requests with a 1,500-request daily cap on Search. One customer request can trigger several searches, and each is billed separately. On the free tier, requests are used to improve Google's products; on paid tiers they are not.</p>

<h2 id="the-2027-price">Why the 2027 price matters more than the launch price</h2>

<p>Google has two promotional price cliffs running at once, and they are often conflated. Carbon and Argon double on <strong>December 1, 2026</strong> — from $2/$10 to $4/$20. The Flash line doubles on <strong>January 1, 2027</strong> — from $0.75/$3.75 to $1.50/$7.50.</p>

<p>Read that second row again. On January 1, 2027, Gemini 3.8 Flash becomes $1.50 input and $7.50 output. That is precisely Hydrogen's price, today, with no expiry. So the practical question for anyone running a Flash workload in November 2026 is not "should I upgrade to Hydrogen for the better model" — it is "should I upgrade now, when the price is the same either way, or in January, when the Flash model's rate goes up to match it?"</p>

<p>Doing the arithmetic on a concrete workload: a pipeline that spends 100M input and 10M output tokens per month on 3.8 Flash costs $75 plus $37.50, or <strong>$112.50 today</strong>. From January 1, 2027, the same pipeline costs <strong>$150 plus $75, or $225</strong>. Switching the same volume to Hydrogen today costs $150 plus $75, or <strong>$225</strong> — identical to where 3.8 Flash is heading, and $112.50 cheaper than staying on 3.8 Flash and doing nothing.</p>

<p>Google is explicit that the Flash promotional rate expires on December 31, 2026, and that the list rate doubles on January 1, 2027. Hydrogen's published rate carries no expiry date in the same table. That asymmetry is the single most decision-relevant fact on the pricing page, and it is not stated in the launch announcement.</p>

<p>There is a floor under the argument. Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite is $0.30/$2.50 with no announced change, and it is the right choice for high-volume classification, routing, and sub-second latency targets. If your workload is a router or a classifier rather than an agent, Flash-Lite remains 5x cheaper on input and 3x cheaper on output than Hydrogen, and it will still be cheaper in 2028. Hydrogen's case is about <em>agentic</em> and <em>coding</em> work that needs more than a Flash-Lite class of capability, not about replacing the cheapest tier in the catalog.</p>

<h2 id="what-changed">What changed from Gemini 3.8 Flash</h2>

<p>Google's migration guide is unusually specific, and most of it is about things that will break if you change only the model string.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>The context budget is now unified.</strong> 3.8 Flash separated 1M input from 64K output. Hydrogen shares one pool across input, thinking, and output tokens. Any hardcoded <code>max_output_tokens</code> below 65,536 should be removed or raised, because it now comes out of the same window as your prompt.</li>
<li><strong>Explicit caching is gone.</strong> <code>cachedContents</code> and <code>caches.create</code> are not supported. Remove them. Implicit caching runs automatically on prompts of at least 4,096 tokens and discounts cached input by 95%, which is why the cached rate is $0.075 rather than $1.50.</li>
<li><strong>Sampling parameters are ignored.</strong> Remove <code>temperature</code>, <code>top_p</code>, and <code>top_k</code>. The server ignores custom values if you send them, so leaving them in gives you a false sense of control.</li>
<li><strong><code>minimal</code> returns an error</strong>, and the default moved from <code>medium</code> to <code>high</code>. The latter is the quiet cost increase: a workload that ran at <code>medium</code> on 3.8 Flash now runs at <code>high</code> on Hydrogen unless you set <code>thinking_level</code> explicitly. Test <code>medium</code> and <code>high</code> against your suite and pick deliberately.</li>
<li><strong>Declare tools natively.</strong> Prompting the model to emit raw-text JSON tool calls with zero declared tools can return <code>MALFORMED_FUNCTION_CALL</code>. Use function calling or structured outputs instead.</li>
<li><strong>Preserve thought signatures across turns.</strong> Use <code>previous_interaction_id</code> in the Interactions API for multi-turn sessions. In client-managed <code>generateContent</code> histories, return prior model turns and thought signatures unchanged, and do not copy one signature across parallel <code>functionCall</code> parts.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="thinking-levels">The four thinking levels, and what they cost you</h2>

<p>The thinking level is the main quality-versus-cost dial, and Hydrogen adds a fourth notch that its siblings do not have. Carbon and Argon default to <code>medium</code> and cap at <code>high</code>. Hydrogen defaults to <code>high</code> and extends to <code>xhigh</code>.</p>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Level</th><th>Token use</th><th>When to use it</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>low</code></td><td>Fewest tokens, lowest latency</td><td>Cost- and latency-sensitive workloads, fast chat, high-throughput processing. Still capable on agentic tasks.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>medium</code></td><td>Lower token use, faster responses</td><td>Interactive coding, live UI prototyping, responsive chat, latency-sensitive tool calls.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>high</code> (default)</td><td>Balanced</td><td>Google's recommended default for most tasks, including complex coding, agentic execution, tool calling, and multimodal work.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>xhigh</code></td><td>Most tokens, deepest reasoning</td><td>Hard multi-step coding and agentic problems where <code>high</code> falls short, when you can absorb the latency and token cost.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Because thinking tokens are billed as output tokens, the level you pick moves your bill directly. Google's guidance is to run your evaluations at all four levels with a generous <code>max_output_tokens</code> so the limit does not truncate answers, then pick the lowest level that meets your quality bar. On a reasoning-heavy task, moving from <code>medium</code> to <code>xhigh</code> can multiply thinking token spend several times over — that is the entire trade, and it is why the default of <code>high</code> is a defensible starting point rather than a free choice.</p>

<p>Google also claims Hydrogen <strong>cuts fabricated claims by more than 5x compared to Gemini 3.8 Flash</strong> by flagging missing context instead of guessing, and that it batches read and search tool calls in parallel and adapts better when tool calls fail. These are vendor claims on their own model, with no published benchmark table behind them in the launch documentation. Take them as directional.</p>

<h2 id="capabilities">Capabilities, and what Hydrogen cannot do</h2>

<p>Hydrogen accepts text, image, video, audio, and PDF input and produces text output. It supports code execution, computer use, file search, function calling, grounding with Google Search and Google Maps, structured outputs, URL context, agentic video understanding, and context caching — implicit only.</p>

<p>The gaps are worth listing explicitly, because several of them rule Hydrogen out for whole categories of workload:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>No image generation and no audio generation.</strong> For images you need the Nano Banana family; for speech the TTS models.</li>
<li><strong>No Live API.</strong> Real-time bidirectional audio is not available on Hydrogen.</li>
<li><strong>No explicit caching.</strong> Only implicit, with its 4,096-token minimum and automatic 95% discount.</li>
<li><strong>64K per synchronous hop.</strong> A single response caps at 65,536 tokens. Past that you need <code>background: true</code> or client-side continuation, and up to 1,048,576 total.</li>
<li><strong>Batch enqueued tokens are the lowest of the frontier models</strong> — 3M on Tier 1, against 5M for Carbon and Argon.</li>
</ul>

<p>One upgrade lands on day one: Hydrogen is now the default model for the <strong>Antigravity agent</strong> in Gemini Managed Agents, which was previously running on 3.8 Flash. That matters for anyone using the managed agent rather than the raw API, because it means the agent's cost profile changes the same moment you adopt it.</p>

<h2 id="code">Calling it</h2>

<p>The Interactions API is the path Google's own guide leads with, and it is where <code>thinking_level</code> lives:</p>

<pre><code is:raw>curl "https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/interactions" \
  -H "x-goog-api-key: $GEMINI_API_KEY" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -X POST \
  -d '{
    "model": "gemini-4-hydrogen",
    "input": "Audit this payment retry queue for race conditions and rewrite the lock acquisition logic.",
    "generation_config": {
      "thinking_level": "xhigh"
    }
  }'
</code></pre>

<p>The Python SDK path uses <code>client.interactions.create</code>. The same request in Python, with the model left at its <code>high</code> default:</p>

<pre><code is:raw>from google import genai

client = genai.Client()

interaction = client.interactions.create(
    model="gemini-4-hydrogen",
    input="Build an interactive single-file HTML token bucket rate-limiter simulator."
)

print(interaction.output_text)
</code></pre>

<p>For long outputs, set <code>max_output_tokens</code> above 65,536 and turn on <code>background</code> so Google's servers continue in 64K-token hops instead of the request timing out:</p>

<pre><code is:raw>from google import genai

client = genai.Client()

job = client.interactions.create(
    model="gemini-4-hydrogen",
    input="Generate a full technical design document for a multi-region billing system.",
    generation_config={
        "max_output_tokens": 200_000,
        "background": True,
    },
)
print(job.id)  # poll for completion
</code></pre>

<h2 id="relays">Where you can actually reach it</h2>

<p>Unlike Gemini Decision 1, which was first-party-only on release, Hydrogen is available through aggregators on day one. The live OpenRouter catalog lists <code>google/gemini-4-hydrogen</code> and a <code>:batch</code> variant, and its per-model endpoint listing is more revealing than the headline rate: Google AI Studio's Flex endpoint serves Hydrogen at <strong>$0.75 in / $3.75 out</strong>, exactly half the Standard tier, with a 943,718-token maximum output. Vertex's US and EU endpoints carry a 10% markup over the global rate at <strong>$1.65 / $8.25</strong>.</p>

<p>Two practical consequences. First, routing to a Flex endpoint or the <code>:batch</code> variant is the cheapest legitimate way to run Hydrogen at scale, and OpenRouter exposes both. Second, the regional endpoints are a reminder that an aggregator's headline "model price" is a floor, not a quote — the endpoint you land on can be 10% more expensive, and a Priority endpoint is 1.8x the base rate.</p>

<p>FreeModel's catalog, by contrast, lists 12 models and all of them are GPT-6.x variants — no Google models at all. If you want Hydrogen, OpenRouter is the aggregator that carries it. Teams running more than one backend can also use a gateway such as <a href="https://portkey.ai/?ref=apirank" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Portkey</a>, whose AI Gateway exposes a single OpenAI-compatible interface across 200+ models and translates between OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google request formats, so a Hydrogen migration does not require rewriting your client.</p>

<h2 id="verdict">Verdict</h2>

<p>Gemini 4 Hydrogen is the cheapest way to hold a frontier-capable model in 2027, and that is a genuinely different proposition from the one Google sold on November 12. The price is not introductory; it is not a rate that expires. The two cliffs running in parallel through the end of the year mean that in six weeks Carbon and Argon will cost 2.7x what Hydrogen costs, and in seven weeks the Flash line will converge on Hydrogen's price from below. A workload that survives on 3.8 Flash today is on a rate that doubles unless it moves.</p>

<p>Where Hydrogen is not the answer: high-volume classification and routing, where Flash-Lite at $0.30/$2.50 is still 5x cheaper and shows no sign of a cliff; hard architecture, security auditing, and scientific reasoning, where Carbon spends more thinking tokens per turn and earns them; and anything needing native image generation, audio output, or the Live API.</p>

<p>The migration cost is real but bounded — the unified context window, the removal of explicit caching and sampling parameters, and the default thinking level moving from <code>medium</code> to <code>high</code> each need a test. The pricing calculus, though, favors moving now rather than in January. If your current bill is pinned to a Flash introductory rate, the decision is not really about models at all; it is about which line you want to be on when the promo expires.</p>

<h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>

<p>Verified on November 13, 2026: Google's Gemini API release notes (the November 12, 2026 Gemini 4 Hydrogen GA entry, its price, the <code>xhigh</code> thinking level, and the Antigravity default); Google's Gemini API pricing page (the Hydrogen, Carbon, Argon, 3.8 Flash, 3.6 Flash, and 3.5 Flash-Lite rate cards across the Free, Standard, Batch, Flex, and Priority tiers, the grounding allowances, and the December 1, 2026 and January 1, 2027 price changes); Google's "What's new in Gemini 4 Hydrogen" guide (the thinking-level table, long-decode rules, migration checklist, and Carbon/Argon pairing guidance); Google's Gemini 4 Hydrogen model page (token limits, capability and non-capability lists, model versioning); Google's rate limits page (usage-tier ladder, batch enqueued token tables, Priority inference multiplier); Google's context caching page (the 4,096-token implicit caching minimum and the 95% discount); Google's Gemini 3.8 Flash model page and "What's new" guide; the live OpenRouter model catalog and per-model endpoint listings for <code>google/gemini-4-hydrogen</code>, <code>google/gemini-3.8-flash</code>, and <code>google/gemini-3.5-flash-lite</code>; Portkey's AI Gateway documentation; and the live FreeModel model catalog.</p>

<p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-6"><strong>Disclosure:</strong> APIRank is an independent comparison site. Pricing data in this article was verified against Google's official Gemini API documentation and the live OpenRouter catalog on 2026-11-13. We may earn affiliate commission when you sign up through links on this page, at no extra cost to you.</p>
`;
